----[Developer Salary, Job Satisfaction & AI Threat Perception Analysis]--------
This project involved conducting an end-to-end statistical analysis of global developer survey data using R to explore 
the relationship between work experience, salary, job satisfaction, education level, and perceptions of AI as a threat to developers.
Using data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, hypothesis testing, correlation analysis, regression modeling, and data visualization techniques, 
the project identified key factors influencing annual compensation and workplace satisfaction among professional developers.
---[Key Activities Performed]-------
	Cleaned and transformed raw survey data using dplyr, tidyr, and readxl
	Filtered and analyzed responses from professional developers only
	Conducted descriptive statistical analysis on:
Work experience
Annual compensation
Job satisfaction
Education level
	Performed missing value analysis and data preprocessing
	Executed hypothesis testing using t-tests to examine differences in salary based on AI threat perception
	Conducted Spearman correlation analysis to evaluate relationships between salary and job satisfaction
	Built:
Simple Linear Regression Model to predict salary from work experience
Multiple Linear Regression Model using work experience, education level, age, and job satisfaction
Logistic Regression Model to analyze factors influencing perceptions of AI threat
Visualized relationships between experience and salary using ggplot2
Compared model performance using the performance package
📈 ---[Key Insights]-------
	Work experience showed a positive relationship with annual salary
	Education level and job satisfaction contributed to salary variation
	Developers’ perceptions of AI as a threat were influenced by professional and demographic factors
	Statistical modeling provided insights into compensation trends within the tech industry
